0.19.624.306 D srv  log_server_r: request:  {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Estimate the probability that the complete original answer contains any span unsupported by its original source. The target always refers to the ORIGINAL source, even when source evidence has been erased. Erasure removes information and does not change the answer's correctness. Return only a JSON object with unsupported_probability and source_sentence_id. Use null when no visible source sentence is a proposed witness."}, {"role": "user", "content": "{\"complete_source\": \"\", \"original_answer\": \"Hillary Clinton surprised the world with the unconventional way she launched her campaign. Not only did she rebrand herself but completely reframed who she is, what she stands for and how she intends to run. Many in the media were critical of the launch, however, from a marketing perspective, it may have been much more successful than critics think. The announcement video changed Clinton's message from being self-absorbed to being empathetic. In marketing terms, rebranding is a strategy to bring a new name, term, symbol or design to an established brand with the aim of developing a new identity in the minds of consumers. Reframing is a strategy that goes further: it seeks to change how a consumer (or voter) emotionally experiences an established brand. Clinton's road trip to Iowa in the van nicknamed Scooby was also part of the campaign and her new and controversial campaign logo was introduced last week. All these pieces brought Clinton back on stage not as a leader from yesterday but, surprisingly, as one for the future. The key to Clinton's success in reframing her message and movement will be consistency.\", \"target\": \"any_unsupported_span_in_original_response\", \"visible_source_sentence_ids\": [], \"source_evidence_erased\": true}"}], "add_generation_prompt": true, "chat_template_kwargs": {"enable_thinking": false}}
